    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "You can now refund a client's unused credit from their page, and `bin/e2e` passes. In the test, a client with a $200 deposit is refunded $50, the page then shows $150.00 available, and the audit log records `REFUND_ISSUED client=1 amount=50.00`.\n\nThe request said \"OfficeFloor server\", but the app is actually Spring Boot (only the Java package is named `net.officefloor`), so I followed the existing Spring code.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Form:** the refund form sits on the client's page, right under \"Available credit\". It has an amount and an optional note.\n- **Limits:** a refund can't be more than the client's available credit. If it is, the server refuses it and the form shows an error.\n- **Where the money comes from:** a refund comes out of the client's unused deposits first, then out of any unused credit notes. Each refund stores how much came from each, so both totals drop by exactly that amount.\n- **Other screens updated:**\n  - The Deposits section's \"Held\" figure goes down after a refund and shows a \"Refunded\" line.\n  - You can no longer put deposit money toward invoices once it has been refunded.\n\n**Changes**\n- New migration `V60__create_refund.sql` adds a `refund` table, and the test reset step now clears it.\n- New `refund` package on the server with `POST` and `GET` at `/api/clients/{id}/refunds`.\n- `ClientCreditService`, `DepositService` and `PaymentService` now subtract refunds.\n- On the front end, the form is in `client-credit.ts`, and the client page refreshes the Deposits section after a refund.\n\n**Not done yet:** there is no on-screen list of past refunds or their notes. The `GET` endpoint returns them, so adding a list later would be a small front-end change.",
